<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Raised transaminases (ALT 88, AST 96 U/L) and platelets of 118 on today&apos;s tests, with spider angiomas and palmar erythema, in a 57-year-old retired stunt performer who received many blood transfusions after crashes before 1992, drinks most evenings and has type 2 diabetes. Hepatitis C antibody with reflex RNA, hepatitis A and B serology, HIV and an abdominal ultrasound are ordered. Please see him within three weeks to confirm the cause, stage his liver disease and plan treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within three weeks; sooner if he develops jaundice, ascites, bleeding or confusion. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does he have chronic hepatitis C, and what is the stage of his liver disease?</item><item>Which antiviral treatment do you recommend, and are there interactions with his medicines?</item><item>Does he need endoscopy or treatment for portal hypertension?</item><item>What surveillance does he need for liver cancer and other complications, and does he need a transplant evaluation?</item><item>How should his pain, diabetes and alcohol use be managed safely with his liver disease?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Knievel, 57, is seen at the request of Dr. Rasmussen two weeks after raised transaminases and low platelets were found at his first visit. He received many transfusions after crashes during his stunt career, before blood was screened for hepatitis C. The hepatitis C antibody is positive with HCV RNA 2.35 million IU/mL, genotype 1a; he has never been treated. He drank three or four drinks most evenings and has cut down to about two since the first visit. He has type 2 diabetes on metformin and chronic pain from old fractures; ibuprofen and omeprazole were stopped two weeks ago. He has had no jaundice, abdominal swelling, black stools, vomiting of blood or confusion. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Robert Craig Knievel was born on October 17, 1938, in the copper-mining town of Butte, Montana, and was raised by his grandparents. [D]</item><item>He began his daredevil career in 1965 with a touring motorcycle show. His crash after jumping the fountains at Caesars Palace in Las Vegas, dated 1968 in the wire obituaries, put him in the hospital, by their account in a coma for a month. [D]</item><item>In 1974 his rocket-powered cycle failed in an attempt to jump Idaho&apos;s Snake River Canyon. In 1975 the crash landing of his jump over 13 buses at Wembley Stadium in London broke his pelvis. In the winter of 1976 an attempt to jump a tank of live sharks in Chicago left him with a concussion and two broken arms, after which he retired from major jumps. [D]</item><item>Guinness World Records lists him as holding the record for the most broken bones in a lifetime, with 433 bone fractures by the end of 1975. His obituaries instead said he suffered nearly 40 broken bones before he retired in 1980. [D]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

