    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Raised transaminases (ALT 88, AST 96 U/L) and low platelets (118) on today&apos;s tests, with spider angiomas and palmar erythema, in a man of 57 who received many blood transfusions after crashes before 1992 and drinks most evenings: chronic liver disease, most likely hepatitis C, must be excluded. Hepatitis C antibody with reflex RNA, hepatitis A and B serology, HIV, liver ultrasound and hepatology review ordered</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R74.01</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem; no symptom or result of his real illness was made public. Superseded at the hepatology consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic hepatitis C, genotype 1a, treatment-naive, HCV RNA 2.35 million IU/mL, likely acquired through blood transfusions after crashes; synthetic modern detail linked to his publicly reported transfusion-acquired hepatitis C</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>B18.2</td><td>S — Synthetic genotype, viral load and timing; his hepatitis C and its likely transfusion source are documented (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Compensated cirrhosis from hepatitis C, with alcohol as a cofactor: liver stiffness 26.8 kPa, FIB-4 4.94, nodular liver and a 14.2 cm spleen on ultrasound, platelets 118; Child-Pugh A5, MELD-Na 8; no ascites, encephalopathy, jaundice or bleeding</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K74.69</td><td>S — Synthetic stage of liver disease linked to his documented hepatitis C and later liver transplant.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Clinically significant portal hypertension in compensated cirrhosis (liver stiffness 25 kPa or more), treated with carvedilol without screening endoscopy (Baveno VII)</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
