    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Sofosbuvir 400 mg and velpatasvir 100 mg, one tablet daily for 12 weeks, once approved; no proton pump inhibitor during treatment; watch for muscle pain on atorvastatin; glucose monitoring for hypoglycemia. [S]</item><item>HCV RNA and liver tests 12 or more weeks after the last dose. [S]</item><item>Carvedilol 6.25 mg daily, raised to 12.5 mg daily if tolerated; no screening endoscopy while he takes it; liver stiffness and platelets a year after cure. [S]</item><item>Ultrasound with alpha-fetoprotein every six months for life, next in July. [S]</item><item>Hepatitis A and hepatitis B vaccination now (two doses each); pneumococcal, influenza and zoster vaccines in due course. [S]</item><item>Complete abstinence from alcohol, with counseling or medicine if needed. [S]</item><item>Teach the signs of decompensation and refer for transplant evaluation only if decompensation occurs or the MELD-Na reaches 15. [S]</item><item>Atorvastatin 20 mg daily; metformin continued; continuous glucose monitoring; yearly eye examination. [S]</item><item>Pain care without oral NSAIDs or opioids: acetaminophen up to 2 g a day, topical diclofenac, physical and aquatic therapy; bone density scan and vitamin D level. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Rasmussen, who saw him the same day. The diagnosis, the stage of his liver disease and the plan were explained to Mr. Knievel, who agreed to treatment and to stop drinking. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Hepatology review at the end of treatment and with liver stiffness a year after cure; Dr. Rasmussen continues primary care, starts carvedilol and the vaccines today, and arranges surveillance. [S]</item></list></text>

